  Hadeeth: { title: 'Hadeeth - Final Exam (Rabi\u02BFah Ibtida\u2019iyyah)', start: '2026-12-01T09:00', qs: [{"kind":"M","q":"Ḥadīth 19: “If you ask, ask Allah.” Which understanding is correct?","A":"It applies to duʿā’ only, and not to requests for worldly help from other people","B":"Asking people for what they are able to give is allowed","C":"It forbids asking Allah for worldly needs","D":"Asking any person for any need is forbidden under all circumstances","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Reliance and tawḥīd."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 35 is correct?","A":"It forbids envy, deceptive bidding, hatred, turning away, and selling over another’s sale","B":"It allows a Muslim to turn away from his brother for more than three nights if he is wronged","C":"It forbids only envy, and allows all of the other acts it lists","D":"It forbids selling at a profit, since profit is itself counted","c":"A","pen":0.25,"exp":"Muslim’s brother."},{"kind":"S","q":"A sister keeps all the obligations carefully and also prays extra units at night. A relative tells her, “You are doing too much; the obligations are enough and anything more is an innovation.” She is unsure whether to continue. Which pair of ḥadīth answers this correctly and encourages her to continue?","A":"Ḥadīth 1 shows that only intentions matter; ḥadīth 38 shows that deeds do not count at all","B":"Ḥadīth 5 shows that extra deeds are rejected; ḥadīth 22 shows that they are to be avoided by all","C":"Ḥadīth 16 shows that anger is forbidden; ḥadīth 17 shows that slaughter is to be done well","D":"Ḥadīth 22: the obligations suffice; ḥadīth 38: voluntary deeds lead to Allah’s love","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Both are true."},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 17, “Allah has prescribed iḥsān in everything” is followed by examples from which two areas?","A":"Prayer and fasting, the two acts the Prophet ﷺ linked with the doing of excellence in worship","B":"Killing (in just retaliation) and slaughtering of animals","C":"Trade and marriage, the two areas in","D":"Worship and dealings between people","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"“If you kill, kill well; if you slaughter, slaughter well.”"},{"kind":"S","q":"A father finds his sixteen-year-old son neglecting the prayer for several weeks. He reasons, “Ḥadīth 34 says whoever sees a wrong should change it with his hand, so I may beat him in front of the family until he prays.” Another relative says he should only dislike it in his heart. Which analysis is strongest?","A":"He should only dislike it in his heart, as he is not a scholar and has no right to correct him","B":"He is wrong, because a father has no authority over a son who is himself a believing Muslim","C":"It depends on ability; the father has authority but must correct with wisdom","D":"He is right, since the first level is the hand and the father may use it whenever he sees fit","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Authority with wisdom."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 20 (“if you feel no shame, do as you wish”) is correct?","A":"The Prophet ﷺ cites it as part of what people still retain from the words of earlier prophethood","B":"It is a ḥadīth qudsī, spoken by Allah in the first person to the Prophet ﷺ, as in other reports","C":"It was first said by the Prophet ﷺ himself","D":"It is narrated by ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and recorded by Muslim alone, with no other collector","c":"A","pen":0.25,"exp":"Abū Masʿūd, al-Bukhārī."},{"kind":"M","q":"Ḥadīth 24 (qudsī) begins “O My servants, I have forbidden injustice for Myself and made it forbidden among you, so do not wrong one another.” What does the opening teach about Allah’s acts?","A":"His power is limited by the rules He has set for Himself, like a king","B":"He has made it forbidden for Himself only in the matters of this world","C":"Injustice is possible for Him but He chooses not to do it out of mercy","D":"His justice is perfect, and He is far above wronging anyone in the least","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Allah is al-ʿAdl."},{"kind":"S","q":"A man makes a very large donation to the mosque. He then tells several people about it, shares photographs, and says that he wishes to be remembered as the most generous man in the town. Nothing he gave was unlawful, and the gift benefits many people. Which ḥadīth explains why the reward of his deed is lost?","A":"Ḥadīth 26: every joint owes a charity, so the donation is already counted","B":"Deeds are by intentions (1): the reward is lost when the aim is other than Allah","C":"Ḥadīth 6: the lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear","D":"Ḥadīth 35: do not envy one another","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Intention."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 16 and anger is correct?","A":"It tells the person to express anger fully, so that it leaves the heart","B":"It forbids the feeling of anger in all circumstances","C":"The command is to control oneself and not act on anger","D":"It applies to anger toward people and not to anger for the sake of Allah","c":"C","pen":0.25,"exp":"Control."},{"kind":"S","q":"A shopkeeper earns part of his profit from interest. To “purify” the rest, he gives a portion of it to charity every month, then prays at length for his business to prosper and wonders why his supplications are not answered. A neighbour says that Allah is generous and accepts all from His servants. Which ḥadīth best answers him?","A":"“Whoever is concealed by a Muslim is concealed by Allah”","B":"“Allah is pure and accepts only what is pure”","C":"“Deeds are by intentions”, and his intention was to give","D":"“Do not become angry”, since this is what the neighbour is doing","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 10."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which pairing of ḥadīth and narrator is correct?","A":"Ḥadīth 19: Muʿādh; ḥadīth 22: Jābir; ḥadīth 40: Ibn ʿUmar","B":"Ḥadīth 19: Ibn ʿAbbās; ḥadīth 22: Jābir; ḥadīth 40: Ibn ʿUmar","C":"Ḥadīth 19: Ibn ʿUmar; ḥadīth 22: Jābir; ḥadīth 40: Ibn ʿAbbās","D":"Ḥadīth 19: Ibn ʿAbbās; ḥadīth 22: Anas; ḥadīth 40: Ibn Masʿūd","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Ibn ʿAbbās (19), Jābir (22), Ibn ʿUmar (40)."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 30 (“Allah has set limits…”) is correct?","A":"It teaches that where Allah is silent the scholars may decide without evidence","B":"It teaches that every matter is forbidden until a text permits it, so that nothing is done without proof","C":"It teaches four categories: obligations, limits, forbidden things, and matters of silence","D":"It teaches that the limits may be crossed when the need is great and no other way is available","c":"C","pen":0.25,"exp":"Do not neglect, transgress, violate, or inquire."},{"kind":"M","q":"The words “whoever covers a Muslim, Allah will cover him” (ḥadīth 36) are best understood as:","A":"Concealing a fault only after the wrongdoer has paid the compensation owed to the one he harmed","B":"Concealing only the faults of scholars and the righteous","C":"Concealing every wrong without exception, including crimes committed against other people","D":"Concealing another’s private fault, but not hiding a wrong whose silence would harm others","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 36."},{"kind":"S","q":"A teacher listens to a student who gives a confident answer about a point of Islamic law in front of the class. When asked, the student cannot cite any evidence from the Qur’ān or the Sunnah and says that it is simply what he feels is right. The teacher wishes to correct him in the right manner. Which teaching supports asking him for the basis?","A":"“Speak good or be silent” (15), so the student should keep his view to himself and say nothing","B":"“Leave what does not concern you” (12), since the student’s view is none of the teacher’s affair","C":"“The religion is naṣīḥah” (7) and “beware of new matters” (28): sincere advice protects the religion from error","D":"“Do not become angry” (16), since the student’s tone is the problem and not his lack of evidence","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Naṣīḥah."},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 14, why is “the one who leaves his religion, separating from the community” killed?","A":"Because the ḥadīth states that all who leave the Muslims must be fought at once","B":"Because he combines apostasy with breaking from the Muslim community","C":"Because apostasy in the heart is punished by the neighbours of the person","D":"Because any person who stops praying is automatically in this category","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"By the court, after proof."},{"kind":"S","q":"A young man in his twenties is invited to be more serious in his religion. He replies, “I will repent when I am old. Besides, the ḥadīth tells me to be a stranger in this world, so I will not attach myself to worship now.” His friend asks which part of ḥadīth 40 refutes his plan. Which part does so?","A":"“Be in this world as a stranger”, which tells him to ignore the world","B":"“Take from your health for your sickness”, which tells him to rest","C":"“When you reach the evening, do not expect the morning”","D":"“Be as a stranger”, since a stranger puts off settling until later","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Ibn ʿUmar’s addition."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 32 (“no harming and no reciprocating harm”) is correct?","A":"Harm is to be removed, and it is not met with equal harm unless the Sharīʿah permits retaliation","B":"Harm is permitted if it benefits the majority of the people, even at the cost of a small number","C":"A man may harm another who first harmed him, up to the same measure, without recourse to a judge","D":"It applies only to harm between neighbours and not to dealings with other people in the town","c":"A","pen":0.25,"exp":"Lā ḍarar wa lā ḍirār."},{"kind":"M","q":"Ḥadīth 15 pairs belief in Allah and the Last Day with three duties. Which set is correct?","A":"Honouring the neighbour, honouring the guest","B":"Speaking good or being silent, forgiving the neighbour","C":"Giving charity, keeping the ties of kinship","D":"Speaking good or remaining silent, honouring the neighbour, and honouring the guest","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Three duties."},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 8 and ḥadīth 14, the phrase “except by its right” (illā bi-ḥaqqihā) refers to:","A":"The right of a ruler to take the property of any person who displeases him","B":"Any act a Muslim considers right in the circumstances of his own situation","C":"The punishments the law prescribes, carried out by lawful authority after proof","D":"The right of a person to take back what was unjustly taken, by force if need be","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Protection and exceptions."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 26 is correct?","A":"It is due only on Fridays, and consists of giving money to the needy at the door of the mosque","B":"Only actions of the limbs count as charity, and speech or kind words are not counted in it","C":"A charity is due each day on every joint, and good words and removing harm count","D":"Only the person with wealth owes it, as the joints are an image and carry no real obligation","c":"C","pen":0.25,"exp":"Every joint."},{"kind":"M","q":"What is the soundest analysis of the shepherd image at the end of ḥadīth 6?","A":"The pasture means the lawful, so the one who avoids it falls into the forbidden","B":"The grazing of animals is itself disliked, and the Prophet ﷺ gave it as a warning","C":"A person must never go near anything permitted, since it may be misused","D":"Acts that approach a boundary lead gradually to crossing it, so prevention is part of piety","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Sadd adh-dharāʾiʿ."},{"kind":"M","q":"Ḥadīth 37 promises a full good deed to the one who intends a sin but leaves it. What is the condition set by the scholars?","A":"He leaves it after a struggle with himself, whatever the reason that brought him to leave it","B":"He leaves it for Allah’s sake; leaving it from inability earns no such reward","C":"He leaves it before any intention enters his heart, so that not even a thought of it was there","D":"He leaves it in the open before other people, so that his leaving can be witnessed by them","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 37."},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 42, “I do not mind” (wa lā ubālī) after “I forgive you for what you have done” teaches that:","A":"No sin is too great to prevent forgiveness once its conditions are met","B":"Allah is indifferent to the sins of His servants, so long","C":"The forgiveness is restricted to sins between the servant and Allah, never between people","D":"Allah forgives without need of repentance for those who have called on Him once","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 42."},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 22 the man vowed to pray, fast Ramaḍān, treat the lawful as lawful and the forbidden as forbidden, and add nothing. What did the Prophet ﷺ reply, and what follows?","A":"He said “Yes”; but only for the first generation who heard it from him","B":"He said “Yes”; voluntary deeds are therefore a sign of weak faith in the believer","C":"He said “Yes”; the obligations with avoiding the forbidden suffice for Jannah","D":"He said “No”; the obligations are not enough unless voluntary deeds are added","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth Jābir."},{"kind":"M","q":"Muslim’s wording of ḥadīth 5 is “whoever does a deed not in accordance with our affair”. What does this wording add to the narration “whoever introduces”?","A":"It extends the rejection to every worldly invention that has no precedent in the Prophet’s ﷺ time","B":"It rejects the deed even of one who merely practises an innovation he did not originate","C":"It excuses the ignorant person who acts upon an innovation because he was never taught otherwise","D":"It limits the rejection to the one who first invented the practice, and spares his followers","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Two wordings, one wider than the other."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which condition is common to the acceptance of both duʿā’ (ḥadīth 10) and charity (the Qur’ān verse in the same ḥadīth)?","A":"The wealth and provision of the giver must be lawful and pure","B":"The act must be done in secret and not in front of other people","C":"The giver must be in a state of hardship","D":"The act must be repeated three times before an answer can be expected","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Allah is pure and accepts only the pure."},{"kind":"M","q":"When a ḥadīth is described as “muttafaqun ʿalayh”, what precisely is meant?","A":"All six canonical collections record it with one and the same chain","B":"Every scholar of ḥadīth agrees on how its words are to be understood","C":"Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it","D":"It reaches the Prophet ﷺ through narrators who all were Companions","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"It refers to the two Ṣaḥīḥs."},{"kind":"S","q":"A man in business prays, fasts and gives zakāh on time. But he speaks harshly to his employees, shouts at them in front of customers and withholds their wages for weeks without a reason. He says that his worship is complete, since the ḥadīth only requires the obligations. Which teaching speaks most directly to his case?","A":"“Righteousness is good character” (27) and the command to deal with people in good character (18)","B":"“Leave what does not concern you” (12), since the wages of workers are not a matter for him","C":"“Obligations suffice” (22), since he fulfils the prayer and the fast without any fault in either","D":"“Deeds are by intentions” (1), since his intention in his worship is good and that is what matters","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Character is part of religion."},{"kind":"S","q":"In the class group chat, a student forwards a story about a classmate that damages his reputation. Nobody has verified it. When challenged, he says, “I am only sharing information; I did not say it is true.” Other students keep forwarding it. Which pair of teachings applies most directly to the sender and to those who forward it?","A":"Deeds are by intentions (1), and the religion is naṣīḥah (7), since sharing was meant as advice","B":"Avoid doubtful matters (6), and ask your heart (27), because a rumour is a doubtful matter","C":"Leave what does not concern you (12), and be mindful of Allah (19), as the latter protects honour","D":"Speak good or be silent (15), and a Muslim’s honour is sacred to a Muslim (35)","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"15 and 35."},{"kind":"M","q":"Ḥadīth 31 (zuhd) advises detachment from the world and from what is in people’s hands. What is the right understanding of zuhd?","A":"Not letting the world settle in the heart, even when it is in the hand","B":"Owning nothing at all and living on charity from other people","C":"Giving up all lawful wealth, as the wealthy cannot be Allah’s beloved","D":"Avoiding marriage and work so as to give all time to worship","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"The heart."},{"kind":"M","q":"Ḥadīth 29 (Muʿādh) names the “head of the matter”, “its pillar” and “its peak”. Which is correct?","A":"Head: Islām; pillar: prayer; peak: jihād","B":"Head: faith; pillar: prayer; peak: knowledge","C":"Head: Islām; pillar: zakāh; peak: the Qur’ān","D":"Head: prayer; pillar: Islām; peak: ḥajj","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"As in the ḥadīth."},{"kind":"M","q":"Ḥadīth 7 lists five categories of naṣīḥah. Which order is as the ḥadīth gives it?","A":"Allah, His Book, the leaders of the Muslims, His Messenger, then the common people","B":"Allah, His Messenger, His Book, the common people, then the leaders of the Muslims","C":"Allah, His Book, His Messenger, the leaders of the Muslims, then their common people","D":"His Book, Allah, His Messenger, the leaders of the Muslims, then the common people","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Order as in Muslim."},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 28 the Prophet ﷺ gave the command to hear and obey “even if a slave is placed over you”. What is the correct scholarly limit?","A":"Obedience in every matter without exception, so that disorder and division among Muslims are avoided","B":"Obedience that applied to the first three generations of Muslims and does not apply to later ones","C":"Obedience only to rulers from the Quraysh tribe, in accordance with the other reports on leadership","D":"Obedience in what is good; there is no obedience to a creature in disobedience to the Creator","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Qualified by other texts."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 12 is correct?","A":"It was said in the context of the rules of inheritance, and applies only to those rules and no others","B":"It forbids a person to help others in their affairs, since helping others is none of his concern","C":"It applies to idle talk and to what has no benefit in religion or the world","D":"It excludes anything that people have not asked about, as only requested help is part of duty","c":"C","pen":0.25,"exp":"“What does not concern him.”"},{"kind":"S","q":"During an argument in the dormitory, a student feels his anger rising. His classmate has insulted his family and is laughing at him. His hand is already clenched and he wants to strike him. Another student asks what the Sunnah teaches in this moment. Which ordered response best matches the teachings?","A":"Shout until the anger leaves him, since holding it in is harmful to the health of the body","B":"Seek refuge in Allah, change posture, make wuḍūʾ, and stay silent until the anger passes","C":"Strike first, then ask forgiveness and give charity so that the matter is not carried further","D":"Leave the place and cut off all relations with the classmate until the anger has fully gone","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 16 and related reports."},{"kind":"S","q":"A man with a long history of sin hears ḥadīth 42 and says, “Forgiveness is promised, even if my sins fill the earth. So I need not repent or change; I will continue as I am and expect Allah to forgive me.” A friend tells him that something is wrong with this reasoning. What is it?","A":"The ḥadīth applies only to those sins that cause no harm to other people, so his case is outside it","B":"Nothing is wrong: the ḥadīth removes the need for repentance for every Muslim who calls on Allah","C":"The ḥadīth applies only to those who have fasted the whole month of Ramaḍān for many years","D":"The promise is conditioned on duʿā’ with hope, istighfār and tawḥīd; it is no licence to continue","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Conditions."},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 13, why does the Prophet ﷺ tie faith to loving for one’s brother what one loves for oneself?","A":"Because faith is annulled by envy, so the envious person is outside Islām at once","B":"Because the “brother” meant is a blood relative","C":"Because love between Muslims is a condition for the validity of the testimony","D":"Because the one lacking this has a flaw in the completeness of faith","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Nafy al-kamāl."},{"kind":"M","q":"What can be deduced from the fact that ḥadīth 16 gives only “do not become angry”, repeated, and no further detail?","A":"Anger is forbidden as a feeling, and whoever feels it has sinned","B":"The command is to control the effects of anger, not to remove the feeling","C":"The Prophet ﷺ himself never felt anger, and so the command is for the followers","D":"The man asking was known to be hot-tempered, so the ruling is for him alone","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"The ruling is about actions."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the ḥadīth of Jibrīl (2) is INCORRECT?","A":"It was narrated by ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and recorded by Muslim","B":"It lists seven pillars of īmān, adding the testimony of faith","C":"Islām is explained through five pillars, īmān through six","D":"The Prophet ﷺ said that Jibrīl came to teach them their religion","c":"B","pen":0.25,"exp":"Six articles of faith."},{"kind":"M","q":"The three-part command of ḥadīth 18 is: taqwā wherever you are, follow a bad deed with a good deed, and:","A":"Behave with people with good character","B":"Give charity in secret to those in need","C":"Keep company with the righteous at all times","D":"Seek forgiveness after every act of worship","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Three commands."},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 21 the man asked for a statement about Islām that he would need to ask no one else. The reply had two parts. They were:","A":"Say the shahādah and then pray the five prayers","B":"Say “I believe in Allah” and then abandon the world","C":"Say “there is no god but Allah” and then give zakāh","D":"Say “I believe in Allah” and then be steadfast","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Faith, then istiqāmah."},{"kind":"S","q":"A seller says he gave a buyer goods and was not paid. The buyer says that he paid in full. Neither has a receipt or any witness, and both are known to be honest. The case is brought to the judge, and each asks to be believed because of his reputation. Which principle settles the case, and who swears?","A":"Both swear and the dispute is settled by dividing the amount","B":"The proof is on the defendant; the oath is on the claimant","C":"The older of the two is believed with no oath required","D":"The proof is on the claimant; the oath is on the one who denies","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 33."},{"kind":"S","q":"A student says to his teacher, “I do not need to ask scholars about the unclear parts of the Qur’ān or the Sunnah. Ḥadīth 27 says to ask my heart, even if people give me a ruling. So I will follow what my heart settles on, whatever the scholars say.” Which reply is correct?","A":"He is right, but only for the matters of dealings with other people","B":"He is wrong: the ḥadīth is weak and is not to be used as proof","C":"The heart is a guide in unclear matters for a trained heart","D":"He is right: the heart replaces teachers, so he need not study","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 27 limits."},{"kind":"S","q":"A man builds a large extension on his own land, which blocks the light and air from the house next door. The neighbour says, “If you will not remove it, I will build a wall on my side to block your light in return.” The first says that since the land is his, nobody has a right to object. Which principle applies to both?","A":"Neither neighbour has a right, because it is outside the matters of the religion","B":"No harming and no reciprocating harm (32); the harm is removed through the judge, not matched in kind","C":"A man may do as he wishes on his own land (32), and the other may do the same","D":"The one who suffers harm may match it exactly (33), as the proof is on the claimant","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 32."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 14 is INCORRECT?","A":"A Muslim’s blood is made lawful for any act of sin","B":"A Muslim’s blood is lawful only in three cases","C":"One of the cases is the married person who commits adultery","D":"One of the cases is a life for a life","c":"A","pen":0.25,"exp":"Only three."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 3 is correct?","A":"Fasting is the first of the five matters, since it is a yearly duty for all","B":"The five include jihād, which Muslims perform together every year","C":"The Prophet ﷺ describes Islām as built upon five matters","D":"Ḥajj is the last of the five, so it is the least important in ranking","c":"C","pen":0.25,"exp":"Five pillars."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which of the following is correct about ḥadīth 4?","A":"The angel writes only the length of life, and the other matters are decided later after birth","B":"The angel writes five things: the four named, and in addition the place where he will be buried","C":"The angel is commanded to write four things: provision, lifespan, deeds and whether wretched or happy","D":"The writing takes place after birth, on the day when the child is given his name by his family","c":"C","pen":0.25,"exp":"Four words."},{"kind":"S","q":"During Ramaḍān, ʿAlī forgot that he was fasting, ate and drank in the afternoon, and then remembered. He stopped, and continued the fast to sunset. A friend tells him that the fast is void and must be made up, and that he is sinful. Which basis is strongest for the ruling that his fast stands?","A":"Whoever intends a good deed has a full reward written for him (37), whatever he does afterwards","B":"Leave what makes you doubt (11), so the fast should be repeated to be on the safe side of the matter","C":"Obligations suffice for Jannah (22), so the fast does not need to be valid in order to be accepted","D":"Allah has pardoned mistakes, forgetfulness and coercion (39), with a report on forgetting while fasting","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 39 and its supporting report."},{"kind":"M","q":"“A mark of the excellence of a man’s Islām is his leaving what does not concern him.” Which of these is NOT excluded by that statement?","A":"Instructing a person who is about to commit a clear wrong, when one is able","B":"Spending long hours on entertainment that brings neither benefit nor harm to the person","C":"Warning a person who is about to wrong someone else, which is the duty of the authorities alone","D":"Asking about the private matters of others with the aim of finding the faults hidden in them","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Duties concern a person."},{"kind":"M","q":"According to ḥadīth 4, when does the angel breathe in the soul?","A":"After three stages of forty days each, as drop, clot and morsel","B":"At the moment of birth, when the child is first able to cry out","C":"After the morsel is clothed in flesh, a further forty days after its completion","D":"At the end of the first forty days","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Forty + forty + forty, then the angel is sent."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 9 is correct?","A":"Both commands and prohibitions are required only within one’s ability","B":"Neither is binding on the person who has not been taught them","C":"Prohibitions are to be avoided entirely, while commands are to be done within one’s ability","D":"Commands are to be done entirely, while prohibitions are avoided as far as able","c":"C","pen":0.25,"exp":"“Avoid it; do as much as you can.”"},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 6, what follows from the words “which many of the people do not know”?","A":"The matter is permitted for the people","B":"The matter is doubtful only for those who lack the knowledge","C":"The matter must be avoided by everyone, even after a scholar has clarified it","D":"The matter is doubtful for every Muslim, including the scholars, until it is decided","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Doubt is relative to knowledge."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which pairing of ḥadīth and Companion-narrator is correct?","A":"Ḥadīth 1: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib; ḥadīth 3: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar; ḥadīth 5: ʿĀʾishah","B":"Ḥadīth 1: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb; ḥadīth 3: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb; ḥadīth 5: Abū Hurayrah","C":"Ḥadīth 1: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb; ḥadīth 3: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās; ḥadīth 5: ʿĀʾishah","D":"Ḥadīth 1: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb; ḥadīth 3: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar; ḥadīth 5: ʿĀʾishah","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Hadith 1 ʿUmar; 3 Ibn ʿUmar; 5 ʿĀʾishah."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 40 is correct?","A":"It says: build your house in this world as though you will live in it for ever and never leave","B":"Be in the world as a stranger or traveller; Ibn ʿUmar added: do not expect the morning","C":"It says: renounce the world completely and live apart from people on the outskirts of the town","D":"It says: take your share of the world and leave the matter of the Ākhirah until old age","c":"B","pen":0.25,"exp":"Ibn ʿUmar’s addition."},{"kind":"S","q":"After a lecture series, a student invents a special dhikr with a fixed number of repetitions, to be recited in a group on a fixed night each week. He believes that it brings great reward and that his intention is sincere. When a friend questions it, he replies that Allah accepts every deed done with a sincere heart. Which is the strongest reply?","A":"Sincerity is one of two conditions; the form must also follow the Sunnah","B":"It is rejected only if it is done in public in front of people","C":"It is accepted, because the dhikr itself consists of praise of Allah and praise is never rejected","D":"Sincerity is enough, since the ḥadīth says that deeds are by intentions and nothing more is asked","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Ḥadīth 1 + 5."},{"kind":"M","q":"How do scholars reconcile ḥadīth 11 (“leave what makes you doubt”) with the rule that certainty is not removed by doubt?","A":"Doubt after a certain state does not change it","B":"The second rule abrogates the first whenever the two meet in one case","C":"The first applies to acts of worship only and the second to dealings with people","D":"Both say the same thing, so there is nothing in need of reconciliation","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Two different situations."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 38 (the walī of Allah) is correct?","A":"Voluntary deeds are loved more than the obligations","B":"Allah declares war on any person who disagrees with a righteous man","C":"The walī’s hearing and sight become independent of Allah’s will","D":"Allah declares war on whoever shows enmity to His walī","c":"D","pen":0.25,"exp":"Obligations first."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about ḥadīth 8 is INCORRECT?","A":"It teaches that the reckoning of the hearts is with Allah","B":"It permits individuals to fight those who leave the prayer","C":"It says that life and wealth are protected for whoever says the shahādah, prays and gives zakāh","D":"It was recorded by al-Bukhārī and Muslim from Ibn ʿUmar","c":"B","pen":0.25,"exp":"Authority only."},{"kind":"M","q":"In ḥadīth 23, what is the meaning of “charity is a proof (burhān)”?","A":"It is clear evidence of the giver’s faith, as only the believer parts with wealth for Allah","B":"It is evidence that the poor have a right over the wealth of everyone","C":"It is a document that the giver may present to be freed from punishment","D":"It is proof that the wealth was lawful, as only lawful wealth is given","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Proof of faith."},{"kind":"M","q":"Ḥadīth 9 closes with the warning that earlier nations perished by “their many questions”. Which kind of question does the warning target?","A":"Questions about the lawful and the unlawful","B":"Every question about religion, including the sincere request for knowledge from the people of the Remembrance","C":"Questions put to anyone other than the Prophet ﷺ himself during his lifetime among the Companions","D":"Questions that press for details of what is not yet obligatory and burden the questioner","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Not a ban on learning."}] },
  Tawheed: { title: 'Tawheed - Final Exam (Rabi\u02BFah Ibtida\u2019iyyah)', start: '2026-12-02T09:00', qs: [{"kind":"M","q":"Which description of the first matter, knowledge, matches the author’s?","A":"Knowing the details of the sciences of the Qur’ān and ḥadīth","B":"Knowing the rulings of Islām with the opinions of the four schools","C":"Knowing Allah, His Prophet ﷺ and the religion of Islām with the evidence","D":"Knowing the names of Allah and nothing besides it","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Definition."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which verse is cited for the purpose for which jinn and mankind were created?","A":"“He it is who created for you all that is in the earth” (Sūratul-Baqarah 2:29)","B":"“Indeed, the believers are those who, when Allah is mentioned, their hearts tremble” (8:2)","C":"“We have created man in the best of forms” (Sūratut-Tīn 95:4), with the command to thank","D":"“I did not create jinn and mankind except to worship Me” (Sūratudh-Dhāriyāt 51:56)","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Purpose of creation."},{"kind":"M","q":"In the three principles, “Who is your Lord?” is answered by which verse, and what makes it a proof?","A":"“Indeed We created you” (Sūratul-Wāqiʿah 56:57): the act of creation is the only proof","B":"“Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds” (Sūratul-Fātiḥah 1:2)","C":"“Say: He is Allah, the One” (Sūratul-Ikhlāṣ 112:1): only the oneness of His essence is stated","D":"“And your Lord has decreed that you worship none but Him” (Sūratul-Isrā’ 17:23): the command to worship","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Fātiḥah 1:2."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which fact about the revelation of the Qur’ān is NOT in the author’s account?","A":"The Qur’ān was revealed to him over thirty years","B":"Revelation began with “Iqra’” in the cave of Ḥirā’","C":"The prophethood is from “Iqra’”, and the message is from “al-Muddaththir”","D":"He was sent with “al-Muddaththir” to warn after prophethood began","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Twenty-three years."},{"kind":"S","q":"A student says, “Allah is the Creator and Sustainer; I affirm this. So nothing more is needed. Worship is a private matter between me and Him, and no outward acts or rules can be demanded of me.” His teacher wishes to respond using the reasoning in Sūratul-Baqarah 2:21–22. Which reply does this?","A":"Lordship leads to the obligation of worship: because He is the Creator, He alone deserves worship, as in Sūratul-Baqarah 2:21–22","B":"Worship is private, so no outward act is required and the heart alone suffices as the proof","C":"Creation is enough, and worship is a personal virtue that is added for the sake of perfection","D":"Worship is for those who are unable to work, and the others may leave it for later in life","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Rubūbiyyah and ulūhiyyah."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which ḥadīth does the author give to prove that hijrah continues?","A":"“Whoever emigrates for Allah, his reward is with Allah in Jannah”, as mentioned in the Qur’ān","B":"“There is no hijrah after the conquest, but only jihād and intention” (agreed upon, Bukhārī and Muslim)","C":"“The best of deeds is hijrah to the land of the pious scholars”, as transmitted by the people of Madīnah","D":"“Hijrah will not cease until repentance ceases, and repentance will not cease until the sun rises from the west”","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Abū Dāwūd."},{"kind":"M","q":"The author distinguishes natural fear from the fear of worship. Which of these is the fear of worship?","A":"Fearing a ruler who can punish in worldly matters, as everyone fears the authorities","B":"Fearing a visible enemy in battle, who can be seen and met in the open field","C":"Fearing a thing in secret and unseen, from which only Allah can protect","D":"Fearing a snake or beast that can harm, which is a natural fear shared by all people","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Khawf as ʿibādah."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the verse “And I created jinn and men only that they worship Me” is INCORRECT?","A":"It shows that worship is the right of Allah over His servants, which they owe to Him","B":"It shows that worship is not valid without tawḥīd, since shirk spoils it","C":"It shows the purpose for which they were created, and that it is to worship Him","D":"It shows that jinn and men were created for the sake of rewarding them with Jannah","c":"D","pen":0.25,"exp":"Purpose."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which ordering of the author’s first three matters of “knowing Allah” is correct?","A":"Knowing Allah, then the Prophet ﷺ, then the religion, each with its evidence","B":"Knowing Allah, then His religion, then His Prophet ﷺ, each with its evidence","C":"Knowing the Prophet ﷺ, then Allah, then the religion, each with its evidence","D":"Knowing the religion, then Allah, then the Prophet ﷺ, each with its evidence","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"The three questions of the grave."},{"kind":"S","q":"At a camping trip, a group of friends watches a full moon rise. One of them proposes that they all prostrate to it “just as a custom, to thank the One who created it”, and says that since they intend Allah in their hearts, no harm will come of it. Which verse refutes this reasoning?","A":"“And the sun and the moon follow a reckoning” (Sūratur-Raḥmān 55:5), which shows their obedience to Allah","B":"“Do not prostrate to the sun or the moon, but prostrate to Allah who created them” (Sūratu Fuṣṣilat 41:37)","C":"“And among His signs are the night and the day”, which shows that they are signs of His power","D":"“It is He who made the sun a shining light and the moon a light” (Sūratu Yūnus 10:5), which shows His power","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"41:37."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which is the author’s proof of the pillar of belief in the Last Day, including the grave?","A":"Only the believers will be raised, and the disbelievers will return to dust","B":"Only the souls will be resurrected, while the bodies will remain in the earth","C":"Mankind will be raised from the dead, taken to account and rewarded for their deeds","D":"All creatures will be raised, with animals judged and then placed in Jannah","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Resurrection and reckoning."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the evidence “Allah has commanded you to worship Him and not to associate anything with Him” (Sūratun-Nisā’ 4:36) is correct?","A":"It places the right of parents before the right of Allah, since they are the cause of one’s existence in this world","B":"It combines the command to worship with the prohibition of shirk","C":"It commands worship of Allah and kindness to parents with equal weight, each being a form of obedience","D":"It forbids shirk only and does not command worship, which is mentioned in other verses of the Qur’ān","c":"B","pen":0.25,"exp":"Order."},{"kind":"M","q":"The author’s definition of the Hijrah and its ruling is:","A":"Moving from the land of ignorance to the land of knowledge, which is only recommended","B":"Moving from the cities to the desert for worship, which suits the pious and the scholars","C":"Moving from the land of shirk to the land of Islām, which is obligatory for this Ummah and remains until the Hour","D":"Moving from Makkah to Madīnah, which ended with the conquest of Makkah in the eighth year","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Obligatory and remains."},{"kind":"M","q":"The author’s third matter says that loyalty to those who oppose Allah and His Messenger is not allowed for the one who affirms tawḥīd. Which verse is his proof?","A":"“You will not find a people who believe in Allah and the Last Day befriending those who oppose Allah and His Messenger” (Sūratul-Mujādilah 58:22)","B":"“O you who believe, do not take My enemy and your enemy as allies, offering them affection” (Sūratul-Mumtaḥanah 60:1) only","C":"“And do not incline toward those who do wrong, lest the Fire touch you” (Sūratu Hūd 11:113), which the author cites","D":"“Allah does not forbid you from those who do not fight you in religion and do not expel you” (60:8), as the sole proof","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"58:22."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the types of tawḥīd as the author expresses is correct?","A":"Tawḥīd of lordship is affirmed by the Makkans, yet they did not become Muslims by it","B":"The polytheists denied that Allah was the Creator, which was their error","C":"Tawḥīd of lordship is enough to make a person a Muslim","D":"Tawḥīd of worship is a matter of the heart alone, not of actions","c":"A","pen":0.25,"exp":"The Makkans."},{"kind":"M","q":"The author’s “five heads of ṭāghūt” include all of the following EXCEPT:","A":"The one who claims a portion of the knowledge of the unseen","B":"The one who withholds zakāh from the poor despite being able to pay it","C":"Iblīs, who is accursed by Allah and calls people to worship other than Him","D":"The one who is worshipped while pleased with that worship","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Heads: Iblīs, one pleased to be worshipped, one calling to worship himself, claimant of the unseen, ruler by other than Allah’s revelation."},{"kind":"S","q":"A man speaks often and passionately about his love for the Prophet ﷺ, attends every Mawlid gathering and spends on it. Yet he regularly misses the prayers and says that love of the Prophet ﷺ in the heart matters more than outward acts. How should Sūratu Āl ʿImrān 3:31 be applied to his claim?","A":"Love is shown by following him; neglecting the prayer contradicts the claim of love","B":"Prayer is for the weak in faith, and love is for the strong, who have passed beyond outward acts","C":"Following is for scholars, while love is for the people, who cannot follow in every detail","D":"Love is enough, and following is only a secondary matter, for the heart is what Allah looks at","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"3:31."},{"kind":"S","q":"Ibrāhīm’s family is celebrating his admission to a top university. His mother vows to slaughter a goat at a well-known shrine “for the blessing of the saint buried there”, and distributes the meat to the poor. When the matter is raised, the family says that they believe Allah alone is the Giver and that the saint is only a means. What is the correct verdict?","A":"It is permitted if the saint is a righteous man known for his piety and close to Allah in his lifetime","B":"It is disliked but not shirk, since the man believes in his heart that Allah alone is the one who gives","C":"It is shirk in worship, since slaughter and the vow are acts of worship dedicated to other than Allah","D":"It is permitted if the meat is given to the poor afterward, since feeding the poor is itself a form of worship","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Dhabḥ and nadhr."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which verse is the author’s proof that rejecting ṭāghūt is part of belief in Allah?","A":"“Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or be silent”","B":"“Whoever disbelieves in ṭāghūt and believes in Allah has grasped the firmest handhold” (Sūratul-Baqarah 2:256)","C":"“Indeed, Allah does not forgive that partners be set up with Him” (Sūratun-Nisā’ 4:48) alone","D":"“There is no compulsion in religion” (Sūratul-Baqarah 2:256), read without its second half","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Both halves are in 2:256."},{"kind":"S","q":"A man who spends long hours in dhikr says, “My iḥsān is that I worship Allah as though I see Him. Because I have reached this level, I do not need to worship in the way the Prophet ﷺ taught, since my heart knows what is pleasing to Allah.” His teacher disagrees. What is wrong with his claim?","A":"Iḥsān is a level of the religion which must rest on Islām and īmān","B":"Nothing is wrong: iḥsān is for the elite, who are above the rulings of the Sharīʿah in their worship","C":"Iḥsān is optional for the common people, and the one who has reached it need not follow the Sunnah","D":"Iḥsān is only about the prayer, so he may follow his own method in the other acts of worship","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Levels."},{"kind":"S","q":"A shopkeeper’s business thrives. He tells his customers, “All this wealth came from the blessing of Shaykh so-and-so; he is the one who provides for me, not my own effort, and certainly not luck.” He does not mention Allah. A listener says that this is just a manner of speaking. What is the issue?","A":"Attributing the giving of provision to a creature as the independent giver undermines tawḥīd of lordship","B":"It is shirk only if he says it in front of other people, but not if he says it to himself","C":"It is only a figure of speech used by the people, and it has no ruling in the religion","D":"It is permitted, since he is righteous and close to Allah and so his blessing is a cause of provision","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Provision."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which pairing of an act of worship with its Qur’ānic evidence is correct?","A":"Istiʿādhah: “When you sought help from your Lord” (8:9); istiʿānah: “Say, I seek refuge” (113:1); istighāthah: “You alone we ask for help” (1:5)","B":"Istiʿānah (seeking help): “You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help” (1:5); istiʿādhah (refuge): “Say, I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak” (113:1)","C":"Istighāthah (rescue): “Say, I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak” (113:1); istiʿānah: “Call on Me” (40:60); istiʿādhah: (8:9)","D":"Istiʿānah: “When you sought help from your Lord” (8:9); istiʿādhah: “You alone we worship” (1:5); istighāthah: “Say, I seek refuge” (113:1)","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Evidences."},{"kind":"M","q":"The author gives which order for events in the life of the Prophet ﷺ?","A":"Born in Madīnah, received revelation at forty, migrated to Makkah after ten years, died aged sixty","B":"Born in Makkah, received revelation at thirty, migrated after thirteen years, died aged sixty-three","C":"Born in Makkah, received revelation at forty, migrated after ten years, and died aged sixty-five","D":"Born in Makkah, received revelation at forty, migrated to Madīnah after thirteen years","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"63 total."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which verse does the author use to show that mosques belong to Allah and so no one is to be called upon in them besides Him?","A":"“Take your adornment at every mosque” (Sūratul-Aʿrāf 7:31)","B":"“And the mosques are for Allah, so do not invoke anyone along with Allah” (Sūratul-Jinn 72:18)","C":"“In houses which Allah has ordered to be raised and His name to be remembered in them” (Sūratun-Nūr 24:36)","D":"“Only he shall maintain the mosques of Allah who believes in Allah” (Sūratut-Tawbah 9:18)","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"72:18."},{"kind":"S","q":"A university student, bored at a party, joins a friend who visits a fortune-teller. He tells everyone that he does not believe a word of it and is only there “for fun”, but he asks about his exam results and listens carefully to the reply. What is the principle that applies to him?","A":"It is permitted if he does not believe him, since the sin lies only in the belief and not in the asking","B":"It is permitted if he asks about worldly matters only, such as trade or travel, and not religion","C":"Claiming the unseen is a head of ṭāghūt; consulting and affirming such claims is forbidden and spoils one’s prayer, per the report","D":"It is forbidden only if the fortune-teller is a non-Muslim, and permitted if he claims to be a Muslim","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Unseen."},{"kind":"M","q":"Al-Bukhārī titled a chapter “Knowledge before speech and action”. Which verse did he use as the basis?","A":"“So know that there is no god but Allah, and ask forgiveness for your sin” (Sūratu Muḥammad 47:19)","B":"“Read in the name of your Lord who created” (Sūratul-ʿAlaq 96:1) as the first revelation","C":"“Say: are those who know equal to those who do not know?” (Sūratuz-Zumar 39:9) alone","D":"“Indeed, the prayer prevents immorality and wrongdoing” (Sūratul-ʿAnkabūt 29:45)","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Knowledge first."},{"kind":"S","q":"A sister has been making duʿā’ for a job for months with no answer. A relative tells her, “Stop asking. What Allah has decreed is fixed, so asking changes nothing and only shows that you doubt Him.” She feels discouraged and is considering giving up duʿā’ altogether. What is the best response?","A":"Duʿā’ is worship and a means within the decree; she is commanded to ask","B":"She is right: asking will not change what was written","C":"She should ask once and then be content","D":"She should ask only for the Ākhirah, as worldly matters are fixed","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Dua."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which verse does the author cite to show that shirk spoils the deed?","A":"“Indeed, Allah does not wrong even by the weight of an atom” (Sūratun-Nisā’ 4:40)","B":"“And We will regard what they did of deeds and make it scattered dust” (Sūratul-Furqān 25:23) alone","C":"“Whoever does an atom’s weight of good will see it” (Sūratuz-Zalzalah 99:7)","D":"“If you associate others with Allah, your deed will surely become worthless” (Sūratuz-Zumar 39:65)","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"39:65."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which statement about the scholar’s words on Sūratul-ʿAṣr is correct?","A":"Mālik held that Sūratul-ʿAṣr applies only to those who lived in Makkah","B":"Aḥmad said Sūratul-ʿAṣr contains the full rulings of prayer and fasting","C":"Ibn Taymiyyah said Sūratul-ʿAṣr replaces the need for the rest of the Qur’ān","D":"Ash-Shāfiʿī said that if people reflected on Sūratul-ʿAṣr alone it would suffice them","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Ash-Shāfiʿī."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which scenario is classed by the author as the “greater” shirk that places one outside the religion?","A":"Swearing by one’s father or by the honour of someone in an ordinary speech","B":"Making a statement of display in a minor act of worship, to impress people","C":"Directing duʿā’ to the dead for what only Allah can give, such as healing or forgiveness","D":"Thinking well of a righteous person without any act directed at him","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Greater shirk."},{"kind":"M","q":"The four matters that the author places at the start of the book are in which order?","A":"Knowledge, patience, acting upon it, and calling others","B":"Acting, knowledge, patience, and calling to what one knows","C":"Knowledge, calling to it, acting upon it, and patience with the harm that follows","D":"Knowledge, acting upon it, calling to it, and patience with the harm that follows","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Order."},{"kind":"S","q":"Before the final exam, a student tells his classmates that he will only memorise the answers to the three questions (Lord, religion, Prophet) as they appear in the book. He says that the verses and ḥadīth proofs are for the scholars and add nothing to his belief. Which reply best reflects the author’s method?","A":"The author gives each answer with its proof, so that belief rests on revelation and not on mere imitation","B":"The proofs matter only for the first two questions, while the third needs only the name of the Prophet ﷺ","C":"The proofs are for scholars only; the student needs the answers to be saved from the questions of the grave","D":"The answers alone are enough for entering Jannah, and the proofs are a matter of added virtue","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Evidence."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about Ibrāhīm’s religion (al-ḥanīfiyyah) is correct?","A":"It is an older law that has been abrogated by the law of Islām","B":"It is a religion for the Arabs alone, who descend from him","C":"It is worshipping Allah alone with sincere devotion, and the Muslims are commanded to follow it","D":"It is a set of rituals which differ entirely from the religion of Islām","c":"C","pen":0.25,"exp":"Ḥanīf."},{"kind":"S","q":"On a trip to the countryside, a group of married couples faces a large tree that locals say “gives children”. They sit round it, recite a dhikr and ask it to bless them with a child, saying that they know Allah is the real Giver and that the tree is only the place where the request is made. What is the ruling?","A":"It is permitted if they intend that Allah gives through the tree, since the tree is only a means","B":"It is disliked only if it is done in front of people, but it is permitted if done in secret","C":"Asking a tree for what only Allah gives is shirk; the matter is to be explained gently and with proof","D":"It is permitted for those who live in the countryside and have not been taught the rulings","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Teaching."},{"kind":"S","q":"After falling into a major sin, a young brother becomes despondent and says, “My īmān has gone and I am no longer a Muslim, so there is no point in praying.” A friend tells him instead that sin makes no difference to īmān at all. Which reply is closest to what the book teaches?","A":"His faith is the same as the scholars’ faith and does not change, whatever he does or leaves","B":"Faith increases with obedience and decreases with sin; a sinning Muslim remains a Muslim and must repent","C":"He has left Islām until he renews the testimony of faith before witnesses and a qualified judge","D":"His faith is unaffected by sin, so he does not need to repent, as faith is a fixed matter of the heart","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Faith has levels."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about istighāthah (seeking rescue) is correct?","A":"Seeking rescue from a living and present person in what he can do is permitted","B":"Seeking rescue from the angels is permitted, if the aim is to ask them to intercede with Allah for him","C":"Seeking rescue from anyone, living or dead, is shirk in every case, because rescue belongs to Allah alone","D":"Seeking rescue from a righteous dead man is permitted, since he is alive in his grave and close to Allah","c":"A","pen":0.25,"exp":"Distinction."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which of these correctly describes the Prophet’s ﷺ lineage as taught in the book?","A":"Muḥammad ibn Abī Ṭālib ibn ʿAbdil-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim, of Quraysh, descended from Ismāʿīl","B":"Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Hāshim ibn ʿAbdil-Muṭṭalib, of the Anṣār, descended from Isḥāq","C":"Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Hāshim ibn ʿAbd Manāf, of Quraysh, descended from Ibrāhīm via Isḥāq","D":"Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbdil-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim, of Quraysh, who are from the Arabs, descended from Ismāʿīl","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Lineage."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which of the following statements on Hijrah is INCORRECT?","A":"It ended with the conquest of Makkah and no hijrah remains for Muslims afterwards","B":"It is limited to the Companions who were with the Prophet ﷺ and cannot be done by later generations","C":"It is required only of those who are persecuted for their religion, never of others in other lands","D":"It ended with the conquest of Makkah, so no hijrah remains for Muslims after that day","c":"A","pen":0.25,"exp":"Continuing."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the second matter (“Allah is not pleased to have a partner”) is correct?","A":"Only an idol is not given a share, but a prophet or an angel may be","B":"Even an angel brought near or a prophet sent may not be given a share in worship","C":"A partner in worship is rejected only if it is done openly","D":"Only a person may not be given a share, but an angel may be","c":"B","pen":0.25,"exp":"No partner."},{"kind":"M","q":"Who among the following does the author say disbelieves because he rejects resurrection?","A":"Whoever doubts the length of the stay in the grave without deciding","B":"Whoever claims that the length of the Day is different to what is reported","C":"Whoever claims that people are not resurrected, per Sūratut-Taghābun 64:7","D":"Whoever denies the intercession of the Prophet ﷺ on the Day of Judgement","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"64:7."},{"kind":"M","q":"The author states that the first pillar of Islām is the testimony. What does the testimony that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah require?","A":"Following the opinion of the scholars in every matter, whatever the evidence that has reached one","B":"Reciting blessings on him in every prayer, visiting his grave, and honouring his family above all people","C":"Giving him a rank in worship alongside Allah as an intercessor for those who ask him after his death","D":"Obeying what he commanded, believing what he reported, avoiding what he forbade and worshipping Allah only as he legislated","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"Four requirements."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the author’s presentation of the “head of ṭāghūt” who rules by other than what Allah revealed is correct?","A":"It refers to a ruler who fails to apply the law out of weakness while believing the law to be right","B":"It refers to a judge who makes an honest error after sincere effort in a case brought before him","C":"It refers to any person who disobeys a legal ruling of the state in a matter of daily life","D":"The text refers to replacing Allah’s law with another and considering it equal or better","c":"D","pen":0.25,"exp":"Replacement."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the relation between duʿā’ and the decree is correct?","A":"Duʿā’ is useless because the decree is fixed, and the servant gains nothing by asking","B":"Duʿā’ is itself worship and a means, and it does not contradict the decree","C":"Duʿā’ is allowed only for the matters of the Ākhirah, since worldly matters are already decided","D":"Duʿā’ is for the weak who do not trust in Allah, and the strong rely on action instead","c":"B","pen":0.25,"exp":"Dua."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which of these is the correct sequence of the Messengers according to the verse that the author cites?","A":"The first is Idrīs, who was the first to be sent to a people, and the last is Muḥammad ﷺ","B":"The first is Ibrāhīm, who began the call to tawḥīd, and the last is Muḥammad ﷺ","C":"The first is Nūḥ and the last is Muḥammad ﷺ, as shown in Sūratun-Nisā’ 4:163 and Sūratul-Aḥzāb 33:40","D":"The first is Ādam, who was a prophet and a messenger, and the last is ʿĪsā","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Nūḥ to Muḥammad."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which description of ṭāghūt is correct?","A":"Only the devils who call to shirk, and not any human being or anything else that is worshipped","B":"Only human rulers who hold power over a country and are obeyed against the revelation of Allah","C":"Only idols made of stone and wood, since the word refers to carved objects of worship alone","D":"Everything that a servant transgresses his limit with, whether worshipped, followed or obeyed","c":"D","pen":0.25,"exp":"Ibn al-Qayyim’s definition."},{"kind":"M","q":"What does the author say worship is without tawḥīd?","A":"It is accepted as long as the person has not worshipped an idol","B":"It is not worship, just as prayer is not prayer without purity (wuḍūʾ)","C":"It is accepted in part, as tawḥīd is the condition of reward and not of validity","D":"It is worship in form, whose reward is deferred to the Day of Judgement","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Shirk mixes with worship and spoils it."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the three questions in the grave is INCORRECT?","A":"They are connected with the book’s three principles","B":"They are: who is your Lord, what is your Book, and who is your Prophet","C":"They are: who is your Lord, what is your religion, and who is your Prophet","D":"The believer answers firmly because Allah makes him firm in the grave","c":"B","pen":0.25,"exp":"Religion, not Book."},{"kind":"M","q":"What do the pillars of iḥsān consist of?","A":"Six pillars: matching the six articles of faith, each at a higher level","B":"Two pillars: worship with love and worship with fear alone","C":"Three pillars: knowledge, action and sincerity of the heart","D":"One pillar: that you worship Allah as though you see Him","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"One pillar."},{"kind":"S","q":"A young graduate is offered a job. The contract requires him to take an oath of absolute loyalty to the director, “to carry out his instructions without question, even where they contradict the commands of Allah”. His friend says that a job is a worldly matter, so it has nothing to do with belief. What principle applies?","A":"The matter is outside belief, since it concerns employment and not the worship of Allah","B":"Obedience to any manager is a duty, since he is the one in authority over the workers","C":"Rejecting ṭāghūt includes refusing obedience to a creature in disobedience to Allah","D":"The pledge is permissible so long as he is a Muslim, because his authority is lawful","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Obedience."},{"kind":"S","q":"A man prays, fasts and says “lā ilāha illā Allāh” every day. Yet whenever he is in difficulty he calls on a dead shaykh by name and asks him to remove it, saying that the shaykh is only a means and that the final decision is Allah’s. A student says that his testimony protects him whatever he does. What is the strongest analysis?","A":"His statement is incomplete only if he asks the dead in public, but valid if it is done in private","B":"His statement is valid for the one who has not been taught, but not for the one who knows the meaning","C":"His statement is nullified by what he does, because asking the dead for what only Allah gives is worship directed to others","D":"His statement stands because he speaks it daily, and the remainder concerns means and not worship at all","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Testimony and action."},{"kind":"M","q":"What does the author conclude from the verse: “And We have not sent any messenger except that he be obeyed by Allah’s permission”?","A":"The purpose of a messenger is that he be followed in obedience","B":"Each messenger brought a message only for the people of his own time","C":"Obedience is due to the messengers, but not to their laws after they pass away","D":"The messengers are obeyed only in matters of worship and not in dealings","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Obedience."},{"kind":"M","q":"Al-Baqarah 2:21–22 is the author’s proof for:","A":"The obligation of worshipping Allah alone, because He alone is the Creator and Provider","B":"The obligation of zakāh, which is paired with the command of prayer","C":"The duty of loving the Prophet ﷺ more than one’s own self","D":"The obligation of the five daily prayers, whose times are fixed","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Rubūbiyyah as the proof of ulūhiyyah."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the necessity of knowledge is correct?","A":"Knowledge is an optional virtue for the scholars, and the common people are excused from it by their lack of ability","B":"Without knowledge, action can be corrupted, as it can happen that one worships Allah in a way He did not legislate","C":"Knowledge is required for a few matters only, such as prayer, while the rest may be done by custom","D":"Action alone is enough, as the sincerity of the heart makes up for whatever knowledge is lacking","c":"B","pen":0.25,"exp":"Knowledge is first."},{"kind":"S","q":"During a class discussion on tawḥīd, a student argues that the people of Makkah in the Prophet’s ﷺ time were already Muslims, because they affirmed that Allah created them, gave them provision and controlled the heavens and the earth. He says the Prophet ﷺ only needed to teach them rituals. What is the best reply?","A":"They were Muslims, but only until the Prophet ﷺ came with his message to them","B":"They affirmed lordship but directed worship to others; this affirmation did not make them Muslims","C":"They were not Muslims because they denied that Allah was their Creator and Provider","D":"They were Muslims because they performed ḥajj to the House and honoured the sacred months","c":"B","pen":0,"exp":"Rubūbiyyah is not enough."},{"kind":"S","q":"A boat is caught in a storm at night. One passenger cries out, “O Shaykh so-and-so, save us!”, calling on a righteous man who died years ago, while his companion calls the coastguard by radio and asks them to come. A third passenger says that both are the same, since each is asking someone other than Allah. Which statement is correct?","A":"Both are permitted, since each of them seeks a means and both call on a creature to help","B":"The first is permitted since he was a righteous man, and the second is shirk because the coastguard is a creature","C":"The first is shirk in istighāthah, the second a permissible means, since the coastguard is alive, present and able","D":"Both are shirk, since each of them calls on a creature and rescue belongs to Allah alone","c":"C","pen":0,"exp":"Distinction."},{"kind":"M","q":"What is the author’s definition of duʿā’ as worship (using Sūratu Ghāfir 40:60)?","A":"“Call on Me, I will respond to you”: asking Allah is worship","B":"“Call on Me” is for the prophets and not for the common people","C":"“Call on Me” is merely advice, and its reward is not specific","D":"“Call on Me” is only for the Day of Judgement and not for this world","c":"A","pen":0,"exp":"Sūratu Ghāfir 40:60."},{"kind":"M","q":"Which verse does the author give for istighāthah, seeking rescue in distress, as worship?","A":"“And when My servants ask you concerning Me, I am near” (Sūratul-Baqarah 2:186)","B":"“Call upon Me; I will respond to you” (Sūratu Ghāfir 40:60) as the sole proof","C":"“Is He not the One who responds to the desperate one when he calls” (Sūratun-Naml 27:62) alone","D":"“When you sought help from your Lord, and He responded to you” (Sūratul-Anfāl 8:9)","c":"D","pen":0,"exp":"8:9."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about īmān in the author’s teaching is correct?","A":"It has sixty-six branches, the highest being the testimony and the lowest being modesty of the person","B":"It has exactly five branches, matching the five pillars of Islām, the highest being prayer","C":"It has more than seventy branches, the highest being lā ilāha illā Allāh and the lowest removing harm from the road","D":"It has no branches at all, since it is a single act of the heart that is either present or absent","c":"C","pen":0.25,"exp":"Over seventy."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement is correct about the Prophet’s ﷺ call in Makkah?","A":"He began with the command of prayer and zakāh before tawḥīd","B":"He called to tawḥīd for ten years before he migrated to Madīnah","C":"He called openly from the first day, with the permission of Quraysh","D":"He called to tawḥīd for thirteen years before the migration to Madīnah","c":"D","pen":0.25,"exp":"Thirteen."},{"kind":"N","q":"Which statement about the testimony of faith is INCORRECT?","A":"It negates the right of worship for anything besides Allah","B":"It is completed by testifying to the Prophet’s ﷺ message","C":"It affirms the right of worship for Allah alone","D":"Its meaning is that there is no creator but Allah, and that is all it requires","c":"D","pen":0.25,"exp":"Not creator."}] }
};
function examSeedRabiah() {
  const msgs = [];
  Object.keys(EXR_DATA).forEach(function (subject) {
    const d = EXR_DATA[subject], t = ex_table_('Exams');
    if (t.rows.some(function (r) { return String(r.Subject).trim() === subject && String(r.Title).trim() === d.title; })) { msgs.push(subject + ': already there'); return; }
    let n = 0;
    t.rows.forEach(function (r) { const m = /^EX-(\d+)$/.exec(String(r.ExamID).trim().toUpperCase()); if (m) n = Math.max(n, Number(m[1])); });
    const examId = 'EX-' + ('000' + (n + 1)).slice(-3);
    append_(t, { ExamID: examId, Title: d.title, Subject: subject, Level: EXR_LEVEL, Start: d.start, Minutes: 75, PassMark: 50, OralMarks: 0,
      Instructions: EXR_INSTR, Shuffle: 'TRUE', State: 'Draft', CreatedBy: 'SYSTEM', Created: ex_fmt_(ex_now_(), false), Kind: 'Exam', Term: schoolTerm_().Key, Instant: 'TRUE' },
      ['ExamID', 'Title', 'Subject', 'Level', 'Start', 'Instructions', 'CreatedBy', 'Created', 'Kind', 'Term']);
    const qt = ex_table_('ExamQuestions');
    d.qs.forEach(function (q, i) {
      append_(qt, { ExamID: examId, QID: String(i + 1), Type: 'MCQ', Question: q.q, A: q.A, B: q.B, C: q.C, D: q.D, Correct: q.c, Marks: 1, Explanation: q.exp, ModelAnswer: '', Penalty: q.pen },
        ['ExamID', 'QID', 'Question', 'A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'Correct', 'Explanation', 'ModelAnswer']);
    });
    msgs.push(subject + ': ' + examId + ' created with ' + d.qs.length + ' questions (Draft)');
  });
  try { ss_().toast(msgs.join(' | '), SCHOOL_NAME, 20); } catch (e) {}
  console.log(msgs.join('\n'));
  return msgs;
}
